  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Patchy fog across SE VA/NE NC will persist through \nmid morning before dissipating. Otherwise, dry, seasonable weather \nis looking likely for Halloween weekend and into early next week. \n\nEarly morning wx analysis shows ~1028mb high pressure centered from \nthe northern Plains to Upper Midwest, ridging toward the area. \nA cold front is approaching from the north. Patchy fog has \ndeveloped across S/SE VA and NE NC. This will expand a bit in \ncoverage during the next few hours, with locally dense fog \npossible. May need to issue an SPS to account for this. The fog \nshould dissipate by mid morning. The cold front approaches later\ntoday with winds becoming NW by the afternoon. However, the \nmain push of CAA will lag behind the front and not arrive until \ntonight. So, expect one more warm day with forecast highs in the\nupper 70s-lower 80s. Winds increase out of the N then NNE \ntonight as the CAA surge arrives. Temps fall into the 40s in \nmost areas, with lower 50s in coastal SE VA/NE NC with the flow \noff the water. Cooler, dry, and pleasant on Halloween, with \nhighs in the lower-mid 60s. Temps fall off quickly (to the upper\n40s-50s) during the evening as high pressure builds over the \narea with lows dropping into the lower-mid 40s by Sunday \nmorning. Slightly milder (upper 60s) on Sunday with more clouds \nahead of an approaching weak shortwave.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances have continued to trend down for early \nnext week, with no precipitation currently in the forecast. Rain \nchances may return by Thursday with above average temperatures to \nstart the month of November.\n\nThe chance for measurable rainfall continues to decrease for Sunday \nnight into Monday despite the passage of a shortwave. While skies \nwill likely be mostly cloudy, low-level moisture will be \nsignificantly lacking. The clouds will keep highs in the upper 60s-\nlower 70s. A warming trend is expected by next Tuesday/Wednesday, as \nupper ridging builds overhead and the surface high moves well \noffshore. Widespread lower-mid 70s appear likely on both days. \nPrecipitation chances finally return to the forecast by next \nThursday-Thursday night as a stronger low pressure system \npotentially tracks from the Mississippi River Valley to the Mid-\nAtlantic/Northeast. However, forecast confidence in this potential \nwelcome rainfall remains understandably low given the timing.",
